		file: "packages/rph-application/src/handlers/validator-registry.ts",
		find: "\t\tbirths: [{ machine: 'ValidatorRegistryEntry.status', statusField: 'status', values: ['ACTIVE'] }],\n\t\taggregateId: p.validatorId,\n\t\tstate: {\n\t\t\t...newEnvelope(command, ENTRY, p.validatorId, { lifecycleStatus: 'ACTIVE' }),\n\t\t\tvalidatorId: p.validatorId,\n\t\t\tsupportedPolicies: p.supportedPolicies,\n\t\t\troleId: p.roleId,\n\t\t\timplementationType: p.implementationType,\n\t\t\trequiredCapabilities: p.requiredCapabilities,\n\t\t\tindependenceAttributes: p.independenceAttributes,\n\t\t\tcostClass: p.costClass,\n\t\t\tlatencyClass: p.latencyClass,\n\t\t\tstatus: 'ACTIVE'",
		replace: "\t\tbirths: [{ machine: 'ValidatorRegistryEntry.status', statusField: 'status', values: ['DISABLED'] }],\n\t\taggregateId: p.validatorId,\n\t\tstate: {\n\t\t\t...newEnvelope(command, ENTRY, p.validatorId, { lifecycleStatus: 'ACTIVE' }),\n\t\t\tvalidatorId: p.validatorId,\n\t\t\tsupportedPolicies: p.supportedPolicies,\n\t\t\troleId: p.roleId,\n\t\t\timplementationType: p.implementationType,\n\t\t\trequiredCapabilities: p.requiredCapabilities,\n\t\t\tindependenceAttributes: p.independenceAttributes,\n\t\t\tcostClass: p.costClass,\n\t\t\tlatencyClass: p.latencyClass,\n\t\t\tstatus: 'DISABLED'",
		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/validator-registry.test.ts"],
		why: "PROMISE, the export's own docblock (validator-registry.ts:35): \"RegisterValidator — create the entry in ACTIVE (the machine's declared initial state).\" The declaration states it twice — `births: [{ machine: 'ValidatorRegistryEntry.status', statusField: 'status', values: ['ACTIVE'] }]` (:46) and `status: 'ACTIVE'` in the committed state (:58) — and the adjacent comment (:40-42) is explicit that the machine's own `initialState: 'ACTIVE'` (rph-domain/src/transitions.data.ts:2332) is corroboration and never the source, because initial states are a fiction on some machines (REG-F-071/REG-F-125).\n\nWHO RELIES: the other four arrows in this same file. `markValidatorDegraded` and `disableValidator` both name ACTIVE among their source states, so an entry born anywhere else has no legal outgoing arrow its registrar can take — it is stranded at birth. Downstream, handlers/assurance.ts refuses `CompleteAssuranceAssessment` carrying a DISABLED validator's result, and the victim's Gate D drives that end to end (:268-280); an entry born DISABLED would bar the results of a validator nobody ever withdrew, while REG-E-024(c) and §35's availability-based selection assume registration yields an available implementation.\n\nWHAT THE MUTATION DOES: it flips BOTH halves of the birth declaration together, so the entry is registered consistently in DISABLED. That choice is load-bearing. The narrower single-field mutant first proposed in triage (flip only the state literal) is a two-conjunct arrangement: `refuseOnBirthDrift` detects the mismatch between the declared birth and the written state and rejects the whole command, so the kill lands on line 63's ACCEPTED expectation and proves only that the object is SELF-CONSISTENT — not that it is ACTIVE. Flipping both satisfies the drift guard by construction and leaves exactly one thing able to redden: the assertion that literally states the promise. It reddened, at line 64, with `expected 'DISABLED' to be 'ACTIVE'`. So a precise assertion on this export's promise does exist, and it discriminates the value, not merely the consistency.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: `Test Files 1 passed (1) / Tests 10 passed (10)`. Mutated: `Test Files 1 failed (1) / Tests 9 failed | 1 passed (10)`. Failure [1/10] is validator-registry.test.ts:64 — `expect(statusOf()).toBe('ACTIVE')` → `AssertionError: expected 'DISABLED' to be 'ACTIVE'`, in the test named `registers in ACTIVE — the machine's declared initial state`. Restored: sha256 back to 86ae62e5679245230ff0558b8963dd68b5a1bec823b83ed17bcdc54a6586db61, 6716 bytes, byte-identical to the pre-mutation file.",
