		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/execution-invocation.test.ts"],
		why: "Its docblock states the promise and names its beneficiary in the same breath: \"Build the context a template will be rendered against, from the work unit's OWN fields. ⚠ THIS DOES NOT REFUSE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A field the work unit cannot supply is simply absent here; `renderPromptTemplate` is the single place that decides whether an absence is fatal.\" WHO RELIES: `renderPromptTemplate`'s `context[m] === undefined` test (prompt-render.ts:66). The absent-not-empty distinction produced here is the ONLY thing that lets that guard tell \"not supplied\" from \"supplied as empty\" — the very precision the sponsor's narrow ruling depends on. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES TO THEM: defaulting an absence to `''` means every gap arrives at the render guard already looking like a supplied value, so the refusal never fires. The model is then sent \"Assess . Deliver .\" — grammatical, plausible, and missing the subject — which is verbatim the render-the-hole outcome the sponsor declined on 2026-09-04.\n\nOBSERVED: Anchor measured at line 100; `assembleStepContext` declaration spans lines 84-103. CLEAN: `bunx vitest run packages/rph-application/src/execution-invocation.test.ts` -> Test Files 1 passed (1), Tests 21 passed (21). MUTATED: Tests 2 failed | 19 passed (21). The precise kill is `CONTROL — a declared field the work unit genuinely lacks is absent, not empty` with `AssertionError: expected { subject: '', expectedOutputs: '' } to deeply equal {}` at execution-invocation.test.ts:85. The second red is downstream, `⛔ REFUSES when the work unit cannot supply a declared placeholder, and says which` at :64 (`expected true to be false`), because the same mutant also defeats the render refusal this function feeds — the blast radius is a consequence of the promise, not an imprecision in the mutant. RESTORED by inverting the edit; sha256 of execution-invocation.ts back to the baseline 983b3cf3…6e38f.",
