		why: "governance.ts:541-547 states the promise: 'A policy governs its OWN waivability (DOC-004 §12; WaiverRule). When the target policy declares waiver rules, the request must satisfy them: the criterion must be eligible under a rule that ALLOWS waiving, and that rule's required compensating controls must all be present -- a waiver may never drop a control to nothing (DOC-004 §12.2 / JCPWA §36.4).' WHO RELIES: the assurance floor, because §16 item 12 forbids by name 'implement waiver as a Boolean', and this check is what distinguishes a waiver of a naming-convention deviation from a waiver of a REJECTED independent Reasoning Review. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES: it weakens 'all required controls present' to 'any control at all will do', so a request naming a WRONG compensating control satisfies the policy's rule. The waiver is granted while the specific control the policy demanded is absent -- the control is not dropped to nothing, which is what makes the defect survive an existence-level check, it is silently substituted.\n\nOBSERVED: PASS=4 FAIL=0 -> PASS=3 FAIL=1 (mutant sha bcba89d23c4cf5512620bf86b1a3c1c6d87837b091d15440e2800b39365d0332). Failing test: 'requestWaiver -- waiverRules enforcement (#1c) > requires the rule's compensating controls -- a waiver may not drop a control to nothing (§36.4)' -- expected 'ACCEPTED' to be 'REJECTED'. Both existence-level siblings stayed green (no control at all -> REJECTED; the required control -> ACCEPTED), so the mutant isolates the PRECISION limb from the EXISTENCE limb. Restored to baseline sha fdfe1c03...; PASS=4 FAIL=0.",
