		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/dwp05-precondition-coverage.test.ts"],
		why: "THE PROMISE (kit.ts:163-171): \"ADMITS LEGAL **or** NOOP — the PERMISSIVE sibling of rph-domain's `canTransition` (LEGAL only). The split is deliberate (JAN-CMDPRE DWP-07 / D2): `advanceStatus` admits the NOOP here at the machine layer and refuses a same-state re-issue ONE layer up, via the command's `precondition` — so re-issue legality is declared PER COMMAND, not baked into the machine.\" WHO RELIES ON IT: every command whose target state can equal its source. `applyTacticalChange` (execution.ts:669-677) is `advanceStatus` with `target: 'ACTIVE'` and `precondition: fromStates('ACTIVE')`, so ACTIVE->ACTIVE passes the precondition and arrives here classified NOOP on EVERY tactical change; decomposition.ts:357 and the execution-step re-issue paths depend on the same admission. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES TO THEM: dropping the NOOP disjunct moves re-issue legality back INTO the machine and refuses it globally — the exact architecture DWP-07/D2 rejected — so a professional can never apply a second tactical change to an active plan, and the per-command declaration that was supposed to decide the question is never consulted. ⭑ WHY THIS LIMB WAS INVISIBLE: the OPPOSITE direction was already held loudly (pwu.test.ts:1186-1192 pins the code AND the refusal message naming the machine), so an always-admit mutant died while the permissive half had nothing at all. That message is `toContain`-shaped and also matches rph-domain/src/stateMachine.ts:91's near-identical string, so it is not usable as this export's killing assertion.\n\nOBSERVED: dwp05-precondition-coverage.test.ts 21/21 PASS -> 20 pass / 1 fail. Killing assertion :292 `expect(tactical(PLAN).status, 'first tactical change on an ACTIVE plan').toBe('ACCEPTED')` -> AssertionError: first tactical change on an ACTIVE plan: expected 'REJECTED' to be 'ACCEPTED'. The reddened test is the one whose NAME states the promise: \"ApplyTacticalChange — the DECLARED HOLD: ACTIVE->ACTIVE is admitted and REPEATABLE\". Measured narrower than the triage feared: the mutant is fail-CLOSED, yet only 1 of this file's 21 tests reddens — every illegal-transition refusal in the file holds, so the kill is attributable to the NOOP limb alone and not to a general collapse. Mutant compiles. Anchor: 1 hit, line 179, OWNERS [\"checkTransition\"] (span 172-186).",
		source: "JAN-PFA S3.3 increment 3 (the remaining 87 bare exports); triage claimed a precise assertion already existed and the RUN adjudicated that claim"
