		why: "PROMISE: a `ZERO_OR_MORE` requirement demands no instance and therefore gates nothing — the exemption Gate A applies through `demandsAnInstance`, reproduced here so the surface's reading of §6.1 and the kernel's cannot diverge. WHO RELIES ON IT: the demo's own sign-off policy, whose SINGLE requirement is `ZERO_OR_MORE` (*\"with this policy's only requirement carrying `cardinality: 'ZERO_OR_MORE'`, `requestAssuranceAssessment`'s required set is empty\"*). WHAT THE MUTATION DOES: every verdict is silently DOWNGRADED to CONDITIONALLY_SATISFIED, so no PWU in the workbench can ever reach a SATISFIED assurance axis — and nothing is refused, so it presents as the product being conservative rather than as a defect. ⚠ A DOWNGRADE NOBODY ASKED FOR IS AS WRONG AS AN UPGRADE NOBODY EARNED, and only the direction differs in how easily it is mistaken for caution. ⭐ RE-MEASURED 2026-10-11 (JAN-EXEC Phase 7 completion E2, ledger rule #5; REG-F-523; REG-D-110 (3)): the evidence predicate is the verdict's ceiling now; the anchor and the guarantee are unchanged. OBSERVED (bun run mutants -- (the E2 seam/verdict/route ids), 2026-10-11, in the builder's worktree, the mutant applied and restored by the runner): KILLED - the runner's verdict, verbatim: 'KILLED         P6b-h-optional-evidence-is-treated-as-mandatory ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯ Failed Tests 1 ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯'",
